## Project Stack
- Java 21, Spring Boot 3.3.0, Spring WebFlux (fully reactive)
- R2DBC + PostgreSQL (no JPA, no Hibernate)
- Liquibase for all schema changes
- Redis for caching (option chain, market intelligence, VIX)
- Gradle build system
- Base package: com.trademesh

## Reactive Rules — Non-Negotiable
- No .block() anywhere in the codebase — ever
- All methods must return Mono<T> or Flux<T>
- Use Mono.zip() for parallel data fetching
- Use flatMap() for dependent reactive calls
- Use switchIfEmpty() to handle empty Mono gracefully
- Use onErrorResume() as the single error boundary per chain
- Wrap Optional<T> around Monos that can legitimately be empty 
  before passing to Mono.zip() — empty Mono inside zip causes 
  the entire zip to never emit

## Database Rules
- PostgreSQL is the source of truth
- All schema changes must go through Liquibase — never modify 
  tables manually
- Create a new numbered changelog file for every schema change
- Register every new changelog in db.changelog-master.yaml
- Changelog naming convention: 
  NNN-create/alter-description.yaml (e.g. 013-alter-oi-snapshots.yaml)
- Never use @PostLoad annotation — it is a JPA annotation and 
  does NOT work in R2DBC (silently ignored)

## Entity Pattern (R2DBC)
Every DB entity must follow this exact pattern:
- Implement Persistable<UUID>
- isNew field: @Transient @JsonIgnore private boolean isNew = false
  (default FALSE — loaded entities are not new)
- Static factory method for new records only:
  public static EntityName createNew() {
      EntityName e = new EntityName();
      e.isNew = true;
      e.id = UUID.randomUUID();
      return e;
  }
- All fields must have explicit @Column("snake_case_name") annotations
- Use @Table("table_name") on the class
- Never use @PostLoad to set isNew — it does not work in R2DBC

## JSONB Fields (R2DBC)
For JSONB columns (e.g. List<TradeLeg> legs):
- Create a dedicated R2DBC converter pair 
  (ReadingConverter + WritingConverter)
- Register converters in R2dbcConfig using 
  R2dbcCustomConversions.of(PostgresDialect.INSTANCE, converters)
- Never use the no-arg constructor 
  new R2dbcCustomConversions(list) — wrong API for Spring Data R2DBC 3.x

## Architecture Conventions — Core Rules
- REST endpoints are READ-ONLY and NEVER persist to DB
- All DB writes happen EXCLUSIVELY through schedulers
- Service classes expose two method variants:
  - Read method (REST-facing): fetches/calculates, no save
  - Save method (scheduler-facing): calls read method then saves
- Never inject repositories directly into schedulers — 
  all persistence goes through the service layer
- Schedulers check TokenAvailabilityTracker before any 
  Kite API call — skip with WARN log if token unavailable
- MarkToMarketScheduler is the SOLE writer of position P&L — 
  no REST endpoint may call markToMarket() or write to paper_trades

## Lot Size — Single Source of Truth
- LotSizeService is the ONLY source of lot sizes
- NIFTY50: 65 lots (verified via live Kite API June 30 2026)
- NIFTYBANK: 30 lots (verified via live Kite API June 30 2026)
- Never hardcode lot sizes anywhere — always use 
  lotSizeService.getLotSize(symbol)
- Config location: trading.lot-sizes in application.yml
- No other lot-size config sections should exist

## P&L Storage Convention
- All P&L fields in paper_trades are stored in POINTS, not rupees
  (entryCredit, currentValue, realizedPnl, unrealizedPnl)
- Rupee conversion happens ONLY at display/alert time:
  rupeeAmount = pnlPoints × lotSizeService.getLotSize(symbol)
- Never store rupee amounts in the database
- Per-leg P&L calculation = (entryPrice - currentLtp) × quantity
  (quantity = lots, do NOT multiply by lotSize here)

## Field Types — PaperTrade / TradeLeg (updated 2026-07-21, BigDecimal migration Phase 1)
- PaperTrade.entryCredit, currentValue, realizedPnl, unrealizedPnl are
  BigDecimal, NOT double/Double. Confirmed and re-confirmed multiple
  times during the migration after repeated incorrect claims that these
  "accept double" or are "kept as double" — they do not, they are not.
  Verify against the actual field declaration in PaperTrade.java before
  ever stating their type, never from memory or inference.
- TradeLeg.strikePrice and limitPrice are also BigDecimal, not double.
- Exit-decision-relevant methods (calculateIronCondorPnl, calculateSpreadPnl,
  shouldExitIronCondor, shouldExitSpread, both markToMarket() paths,
  isLegBreach()) must use full BigDecimal arithmetic throughout —
  .subtract()/.add()/.multiply()/.compareTo(), never round-trip through
  double mid-calculation (BigDecimal → double → BigDecimal is a real,
  repeated mistake this project has made — it compiles but silently
  reintroduces the precision loss the migration exists to prevent).
- Approved double-boundary exceptions (may convert to double internally,
  since they are read-only display paths with zero exit-decision
  consumers): calculateLiveUnrealizedPnl(), calculateIronCondorCurrentValue(),
  calculateSpreadCurrentValue(), calculateDefaultCurrentValue().
- JSON API serialization: @JsonFormat(pattern = "0.00") does NOT
  reliably format BigDecimal — confirmed via live API testing, it still
  emits raw database column scale (e.g. 21.2000, not 21.20). Use a
  custom @JsonSerialize(using = BigDecimalTwoDecimalSerializer.class)
  instead (calls value.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP) then
  gen.writeNumber()). Verify serialization behavior via direct curl
  against a running instance — Swagger UI's displayed output has been
  confirmed unreliable for this specific check (shows cached/normalized
  formatting inconsistent with the raw wire response).

## Market Intelligence Freshness
- generateIntelligence(symbol) — uses Redis cache (5 min TTL)
  Use for: read-only endpoints, monitoring, display
- generateFreshIntelligence(symbol) — bypasses cache, always fresh
  Use for: TradingOrchestrator.runTradingCycle() ONLY
  (any path that makes trade entry decisions)
- Never use generateIntelligence() in the trade-execution path

## Strategy Configuration
All strategies configured in application.yml under
trading-strategies.items as a list.
Each strategy has shared fields plus an extra map for
strategy-specific parameters.
StrategyProperties has getExtraDouble(key, default) and
getExtraInt(key, default) helper methods.
Strategy-specific extra params go in the extra map —
never add strategy-specific fields to StrategyProperties directly.

## Strategy Package Structure
- com.trademesh.strategy.expiryday — ExpiryDayStrategy
- com.trademesh.strategy.ironcondor — IronCondorStrategy,
  IronCondorSignal
- com.trademesh.strategy.spread — BullPutSpreadStrategy,
  BearCallSpreadStrategy

## Strike Rounding
Always round strikes to valid exchange steps:
- NIFTY50: step = 50 points
- NIFTYBANK: step = 100 points
Use roundUpToStep() / roundDownToStep() helpers
(available in ExpiryDayStrategy, reuse don't duplicate)

## Expiry Date Detection
findNearestWeeklyExpiry() uses !date.isBefore(LocalDate.now())
NOT date.isAfter(LocalDate.now())
This ensures today's expiry is included on expiry day.
Do not change this back to isAfter() — it was a confirmed
production bug.

## Logging
- Use structured logging (SLF4J + Lombok @Slf4j)
- Never use System.out.println
- Business events: INFO
- Intermediate calculations: DEBUG
- Skipped operations: INFO with reason
- Unexpected states: WARN
- Exceptions: ERROR with full stack trace

## Javadoc Style
Concise multi-line format:
/**
 * One-line description.
 *
 * @param name description
 * @return description
 */
No HTML tags in Javadoc.

## Code Generation Rules
- Generate production-ready code only
- No placeholder implementations — implement fully or not at all
- No TODO comments unless explicitly requested
- All BigDecimal arithmetic uses BigDecimal methods or converts
  to double for Math functions then back to BigDecimal
- Use RoundingMode.HALF_UP for all BigDecimal rounding
- Use ZoneId.of("Asia/Kolkata") for all IST time operations

## Testing
- Unit tests required for business logic
- Integration tests for controllers and repositories
- Never skip tests to make build pass — fix the actual issue
- Run diagnostic/debug commands directly and simply — one focused command per question, not chained multi-stage filter pipelines. If output needs isolating, narrow the test scope (--tests "ClassName.methodName") rather than filtering broad output after the fact.
- If a command produces an unexpected result (empty output, no match, silent failure), the next action must be diagnosis, not retry: state a specific hypothesis for why the result was unexpected, then check exactly one thing to test that hypothesis. Never run a second variant of the same command hoping different filtering produces a different result. If two consecutive diagnosis attempts don't resolve the confusion, stop and report the unexplained behavior honestly rather than continuing to retry.
- Never trust a completion report (from Cline or any tool) that a file was edited, created, or deleted without independently re-verifying the actual result — re-read the file, re-run grep, or re-check the filesystem directly. A reported success and an actual success are not the same thing; this has been confirmed to diverge multiple times, including a silently-skipped edit within a larger multi-part task with no error raised.
- A mixed-type ternary (X != null ? X.getSomeBigDecimalMethod() : 0) with
  a bare int/double literal on the null branch compiles cleanly but can
  fail at RUNTIME — Java's ternary type inference silently resolves the
  mixed-type branches to a boxed numeric type, which then breaks
  String.format's %.2f/%f specifiers with IllegalFormatConversionException
  only when the null branch actually fires. This is invisible to
  compileJava/compileTestJava entirely. When converting any field to
  BigDecimal, grep specifically for this ternary shape
  ("!= null ? X.getY() : 0") and fix the fallback to BigDecimal.ZERO
  (or the correctly-typed equivalent), not just direct setter/comparison
  call sites.
- A file-count or error-count grep against gradlew compile output can be
  silently truncated by the compiler's default error cap (observed:
  showed "first 100 of 166 total"). Never treat a grep-derived file list
  as complete without confirming the run wasn't capped — re-run with an
  explicit uncapped flag or independently verify the count another way
  (e.g. sort -u | wc -l against a rerun) before treating a file list as
  final scope for a multi-file fix.

## Verification Protocol (added 2026-07-26, Phase 1 session)
- Cline is NEVER asked to produce verification of its own work — no
  diffs, no build output, no test output, no completion/success status.
  All verification is performed by Kuldeep independently, using commands
  given to him directly, outside of Cline's task instructions.
- Every task given to Cline is scoped to code changes only, and ends with
  an explicit instruction not to report completion, success, or a
  "done"/"clean"/"verified" status.
- A narrative description of what Cline believes it did (e.g. "all four
  files have been corrected," "fields are already present," "ready for
  compilation verification") is never treated as evidence — it is treated
  as an unverified claim, exactly like any other claim in this project's
  evidence-first culture, until confirmed by a real command run by
  Kuldeep.
- This rule exists because of a real incident (2026-07-26, Phase 1
  session): Cline stated fields "are already present" and that a prior
  deletion was reverted "without deleting existing fields," in the same
  message where the actual `git diff`/`grep` output — already pasted by
  Kuldeep in the prior turn — directly disproved the claim. This was not
  an optimistic gap in an otherwise-honest report; it was a direct false
  statement contradicted by evidence already in front of both parties.
- Reviews proceed one file at a time via real `git diff` output, never in
  batch, even when Cline reports multiple files as clean together in one
  summary. A "clean" claim about File B is not evidence about File B just
  because File A's diff, reviewed separately, happened to be clean.
- Deeply nested reactive method chains (multiple stacked `flatMap`/lambda
  scopes) cannot be fully verified via diff review alone — variable
  scoping errors across lambda boundaries are not always visible in a
  unified diff. A real `./gradlew compileJava`/`compileTestJava` run is
  required before trusting such a change, even after a diff looks correct
  on read-through. Confirmed incident: a local-variable scoping bug in
  `IronCondorStrategy.java` during Phase 1 passed diff review cleanly and
  only surfaced via a real compile (`cannot find symbol`).

## What NOT to do (confirmed production bugs — never repeat)
- Never use @PostLoad in R2DBC entities
- Never call markToMarket() from a REST endpoint
- Never store rupee amounts in paper_trades fields
- Never hardcode lot sizes
- Never use isAfter(today) for expiry date detection
- Never use new R2dbcCustomConversions(list) — use .of() factory
- Never use Mono.defaultIfEmpty(null) — throws NPE in reactor
- Never add a fixed-offset widening to OI-wall strikes —
  use ATR-scaled boundary with principled fallback (Option C)
- Never cache trade-decision intelligence — use generateFreshIntelligence()
- Never hand-calculate expected test values without verifying against real computed output first (debug print + isolated run) — repeated arithmetic errors in test fixtures this session cost multiple debugging rounds each time this was skipped.
- Never assume the current shell context (WSL bash, PowerShell, Git Bash/MINGW64) without confirming it — commands that work in one fail silently or loudly in another (heredocs, `which` vs `Get-Command`, path separators, quoting rules for multi-line strings all differ). Specify which shell a command targets when giving one; when receiving one, confirm which shell is active before running it if unclear.
- Never claim a PaperTrade or TradeLeg P&L/price field "accepts double" or is "kept as double" — entryCredit, currentValue, realizedPnl, unrealizedPnl, strikePrice, and limitPrice are all BigDecimal as of the 2026-07-21 migration. This exact false claim was made and had to be corrected three separate times on the same file in one session.
- Never round-trip a BigDecimal through double mid-calculation in exit-decision code (BigDecimal.valueOf(x.doubleValue())) — it compiles and looks like a valid conversion but silently discards the precision the BigDecimal migration exists to preserve. Only convert to double at an approved, explicitly-scoped display-only boundary (see Field Types section above), never inside calculateIronCondorPnl/calculateSpreadPnl/shouldExitIronCondor/shouldExitSpread/markToMarket/isLegBreach.
- Never rely on @JsonFormat(pattern = "0.00") to format a BigDecimal field for JSON output — it does not reliably apply. Use a custom @JsonSerialize-based serializer instead (see Field Types section above).
- Never let an "additive" field-addition task use a full-file rewrite (write_to_file) instead of a targeted edit (replace_in_file/str_replace) — a full rewrite can silently delete unrelated existing fields, methods, or annotations while looking like a small diff. Confirmed incident (2026-07-26, Phase 1 session): a rewrite of PaperTrade.java deleted status, updatedAt, tradeDate, createdAt, maxLoss, and stripped @JsonSerialize/@JsonIgnoreProperties annotations, while the task was scoped to adding 6 new nullable fields.
- Never let an entry-time value (e.g. stopDistancePoints, targetThreshold, plannedRiskPerLot) be computed independently in two places instead of once and passed/copied. Two independent computations of a related value is the exact defect class behind the real 2026-07-24 shouldExitSpread() production bug, and recurred during the Phase 1 session (a dead, duplicated computation block in ExpiryDayStrategy.java, caught and removed before merge).
- Never assume a guard condition (e.g. status != TradeStatus.OPEN) is safe to silently replace with a differently-shaped equivalent (e.g. exitTime != null) as a side effect of an unrelated task. Confirmed near-incident (2026-07-26, Phase 1 session): an unrequested refactor of exit-path guards also silently deleted closeIronCondorTrade()'s trade.setStatus(TradeStatus.CLOSED) line, which would have permanently blocked all future Iron Condor entries the first time a trade closed through that path.
- Never assume all strategies sharing a conceptual category (e.g. "the three-gate risk architecture applies to all four strategies") actually route through the same code path — confirm the real dispatch logic first. Confirmed incident (2026-07-27, Phase 2 session): EXPIRY_DAY_STRANGLE does not use shouldExitIronCondor()/shouldExitSpread() at all — markToMarket()'s dispatch falls it into a completely separate "default" branch using determineMarkToMarketExitTrigger(), a hardcoded currentValue-ratio check unrelated to cfg.getStopLossPercent()/getTargetProfitPercent() or the frozen stopDistancePoints/targetThreshold fields. Caught by reading the actual dispatch code before writing any Cline prompt, not after — this is the check to repeat before scoping any future change described as applying "to all strategies."
- Never fabricate test input data (mock spot prices, strike prices, etc.) without checking it's internally consistent with unrelated checks earlier in the same method. Confirmed incident (2026-07-27, Phase 2 session): a new exit-logic test used a mocked spot price of 24000 against an Iron Condor short call strike of 19400 — internally inconsistent, since 24000 > 19400 triggers a real, correct LEG_BREACH before the test's intended stop-loss/target checks ever run. 3 of 6 tests failed on first run for this reason; only caught via an actual test execution, not diff review.
- Never accept a first-draft "integration test" that reimplements the formula/logic under test locally instead of calling the real production method. Confirmed incident (2026-07-28, Phase 3 session): the first draft of three new strategy test files never instantiated IronCondorStrategy/BullPutSpreadStrategy/BearCallSpreadStrategy and never called evaluate() anywhere — every test recomputed the ATR multiplier formula as a local calculation and asserted the reimplementation was internally consistent with itself. These tests would have kept passing even if the real production formula were wrong, reverted, or deleted. Caught by inspection, not a failing run — a well-formed, green test file is not evidence it tests anything real; check whether the class under test is ever actually instantiated and its real method actually called.
- Never trust a hardcoded assumption about what a shared mock fixture (e.g. a synthetic option chain) produces as input to real strategy logic — verify it against actual computed output, not hand-calculation. Confirmed incident (2026-07-28, Phase 3 session): netCredit was assumed to be 40 in every new Phase 3 test based on a hand-trace of the mock option chain's LTP curve; the real value (confirmed via actual Mockito invocation logs) was 32 for Iron Condor and different again for each spread strategy (8.0 for Bull Put Spread) — each wrong assumption caused cascading test failures (wrong expected values, then a real min-credit-pct gate rejection once the confidence gate was separately fixed) that took multiple rounds to fully resolve. When a hand-calculation and real logged output disagree, trust the real output and re-derive the hand-calculation's error, not the reverse.
- Never mix Mockito ArgumentMatchers (any(), argThat(), eq()) with raw literal values in the same stubbed call — Mockito requires ALL arguments to be matchers if ANY of them is, and mixing throws InvalidUseOfMatchersException ("N matchers expected, 1 recorded"). Confirmed incident (2026-07-28, Phase 3 session): a fix attempt combined argThat() on one argument with raw values (vixValue, 75, 51) on the others, causing this exact exception across all 3 affected tests. Fix by wrapping every argument in a matcher — use eq(...) for arguments that should still match exactly.
- Never guess at a stale mock's correct replacement value without direct evidence when a test fails silently (e.g. an int-returning mock method returning its default of 0 rather than throwing). Confirmed incident (2026-07-28, Phase 3 session): after a sizing-input swap made a pre-existing test's getPositionSize() stub stop matching, a hand-calculated "corrected" value (2600) was still wrong; only a temporary thenAnswer() probe printing the actual argument Mockito received revealed the true value (2080). When a stub silently fails to match (no "Argument(s) are different!" diff shown), add a temporary debug probe to capture the real invocation rather than iterating on further guesses.
- NEVER write a comment, commit message, or explanation claiming the user confirmed or approved a design decision that was not actually confirmed in the conversation. This is a serious integrity violation, not an ordinary implementation mistake — distinct from and worse than a wrong guess. Confirmed incident (2026-07-29, Expiry Day hedge Phase 1 session): a code comment read "// Use call hedge distance for wing sizing (confirmed with user)" when the user had explicitly said the opposite (log both sides separately, don't pick one) earlier in the same task. If a design choice needs to be made and wasn't explicitly specified, stop and ask — never decide unilaterally and then mislabel the decision as user-approved.
- Never report a task as "complete" when part of the explicit scope was silently deferred to a later phase, even if the deferral is disclosed somewhere in the same message. Confirmed incident (2026-07-29, Expiry Day hedge Phase 1 session): a "Phase 1 Implementation Complete" summary included, buried in the file-by-file breakdown, "buildTradeLegs() updated: Includes Phase 1 shadow logging comment (actual logging wiring deferred to Phase 2)" — meaning the literal core purpose of the phase (wiring and running real shadow logging) was replaced with a comment, while the overall framing still claimed completion. If a genuine blocker prevents completing a scoped item, stop and report the specific blocker as the headline, not a subordinate detail inside a "complete" narrative.
- When testing a private inner class/method that has no suitable public entry point, reflection is an acceptable, legitimate approach (does not count as the "reimplemented formula" anti-pattern) — but confirm the target class/method's actual accessibility first. Confirmed incident (2026-07-29, Expiry Day hedge Phase 1 session): a new test needed to construct and inspect pre-existing inner classes (StrikeSelection, TradeLegsResult) that were declared `private static class`, causing real compile errors ("has private access") not caught until an actual compile was run. Fixed by changing visibility to package-private, matching the already-correct visibility of a new sibling class added in the same change. Check existing class visibility before assuming a new test can reach it.
- A regression test's name and docstring claiming to test method X does not mean it tests method X — verify by reading what method the test body actually invokes. Confirmed incident (2026-07-29, Expiry Day hedge Phase 1 session): a test titled "Regression — buildTradeLegs() return identical with/without hedge strikes" never once called buildTradeLegs() — it only checked that a different, unrelated method (selectHedgeStrikes()) was deterministic across two calls. This is a weaker, different claim than what the name promised. When a test claims to prove "X's behavior is unaffected by Y," confirm X itself is actually invoked in the test body, not a component of X in isolation. When an existing sibling test file already has a working pattern for constructing/mocking the class under test (checked here: ExpiryDayStrategyPositionSizingTest.java), reuse that pattern rather than inventing a new, less complete one.
- Do not trust hand-guessed distances/thresholds for option Greeks near very short time-to-expiry (0DTE/same-day) — delta collapses far more sharply with distance-from-spot than intuition built on multi-day option experience suggests. Confirmed incident (2026-07-29, Expiry Day hedge Phase 1 session): test data assumed strikes 100-300 points away would show delta near 0.02-0.05 at tte=1 day; real computed values showed delta still at 0.42 at 100 points and only collapsing to 0.026 by 500 points. Always compute (via a temporary debug print or direct calculation) rather than estimate Greek values for any near-0DTE scenario before writing test assertions or design assumptions around them.
- Never trust a hand-derived option Greek (delta, etc.) over a real call to the actual production method, even after a first hand-derivation was already corrected once. Confirmed incident (2026-07-30, Expiry Day hedge Phase 2 session): after the 0DTE-collapse lesson above was already known, a manual Black-Scholes re-derivation was attempted anyway to save time, produced a sign/direction error on the put side (claimed put deltas near 0.9998 at 100-500 points, implying OTM puts behave like deep ITM — physically backwards), and this wrong result was "corrected" into an even more wrong ~3000-point call/put asymmetry claim before being caught. Both were discarded only after writing a real JUnit test that called ImpliedVolatilityCalculator.delta() directly and printing genuine output, which showed a symmetric, sane 200-point distance on both sides. If a Greek value is needed for test data or a design decision, call the real method — never re-derive the formula by hand, even as a "quick check," even under time/token pressure.
- Mockito mocks silently return the language's default value (0, 0.0, null, false) for any method not explicitly stubbed with when(...).thenReturn(...) — they do NOT execute the real method body, so a hardcoded fallback written inside the real method (e.g. getExtraDouble(key, 150.0)'s "150.0" default) never actually applies to a mock. Confirmed incident (2026-07-30, Expiry Day hedge Phase 2 session): createMockConfig() never stubbed getExtraDouble("hedge-min-distance-points", ...) or getExtraDouble("hedge-target-delta", ...); the mock silently returned 0.0 for both, letting a too-close hedge strike (distance=100) incorrectly pass the intended 150-point minimum-distance floor. The bug produced a real, non-obvious downstream number (maxLossPerLotHedged=1235.0 instead of the expected ~7676.5) that took a temporary log.error probe to trace back to its actual cause. Always explicitly stub every config/threshold key a method under test actually reads — do not assume an unstubbed key "falls back" to anything.
- When comparing a real, double-derived computed value (wrapped via BigDecimal.valueOf(double)) against a test's independently-computed exact BigDecimal expected value, use a small-tolerance comparison, not exact BigDecimal.compareTo() == 0. Confirmed incident (2026-07-30, Expiry Day hedge Phase 2 session): production code's double arithmetic produced maxLossPerLotHedged=7676.5000000000000975 (floating-point noise) against a test's exact BigDecimal computation landing on 7676.5000000000000000 — these are effectively equal but compareTo() treats them as different, causing a spurious test failure that looked identical to a real logic bug until traced. This codebase's established convention elsewhere in the same test files is a 0.01 tolerance; match it rather than reintroducing exact comparison.
- When System.out.println or file-write (Files.writeString) output from a test is not reliably visible through the current tool/shell pipeline, do not keep retrying the same capture method — switch to embedding the real value in a JUnit assertion failure message (assertEquals with a deliberately wrong expected value, or Assertions.fail(...) with the real value interpolated into the message string) and read it back from the JUnit XML report (build/test-results/test/TEST-*.xml, message="..." attribute) via Select-String/grep. Confirmed reliable this session (2026-07-30, Expiry Day hedge Phase 2 session) after multiple failed attempts at stdout and file-based capture.
- Never trust Cline's self-reported "tests passing," "exit code 0," or "build successful" claims — always require and independently run the actual verification command and inspect the raw output directly. Confirmed incident (2026-07-30, Expiry Day hedge Phase 2 session): Cline reported "Test execution shows exit code 0 (success)" multiple separate times in one session when the real, independently-run ./gradlew clean test command showed BUILD FAILED with specific test failures — including once immediately after a debug/verification lambda had been left in place of a real assertion (always-true argThat matcher), meaning the reported "pass" would have been true regardless of correctness even if honestly reported. Standing rule: Cline prompts are for code changes only; every verification step (test runs, git diff, git status, git log) is run directly by the developer, with results read from real command output, never accepted as a narrative claim from Cline.
- After using a temporary debug/verification construct in a test (a println lambda, an always-true argThat() matcher, a forced-fail assertion used to print real values) to diagnose a root cause, explicitly verify it was replaced with the real assertion afterward — do not assume "debug logging added" also means "real check restored." Confirmed incident (2026-07-30, Expiry Day hedge Phase 2 session): a debug argThat(arg -> { System.out.println(...); return true; }) added to trace a real value replaced the test's actual verification (argThat(arg -> arg.compareTo(expectedMaxLoss) == 0)) entirely rather than being removed after use — the test would have passed with any computed value, correct or wrong, until caught by reading the real diff rather than trusting a "verified passing" summary.
- When re-scoping a design decision described as "do X the same way strategy Y already does," verify what Y's mechanism actually is by re-reading real code/history rather than trusting a remembered summary of it — a plausible-sounding paraphrase can be subtly wrong. Confirmed incident (2026-07-31, Expiry Day hedge Phase 3 session): an initial recommendation to migrate Expiry Day Strangle's exit trigger onto "a maxLossPerLotHedged-based structural threshold" (implying a direct comparison against the wing-width sizing input) turned out to misdescribe Iron Condor's actual mechanism — its real STOP_LOSS/TARGET_HIT check is a separate percentage-of-credit formula (stopDistancePoints/targetThreshold), not the sizing input at all. Caught and corrected before any code was written by re-reading the real Phase 2/3 history rather than proceeding on the first plausible-sounding framing.
- Do not reuse a rupee-denominated (× lotSize) value directly in a points-denominated comparison, or vice versa, even when both values conceptually represent "risk per lot" — always confirm units match before wiring one into a new comparison. Confirmed near-miss, caught before implementation (2026-07-31, Expiry Day hedge Phase 3 session): maxLossPerLotHedged is rupees (× lotSize baked in, correct only for feeding lotSizeService.getPositionSize()), while calculateExpiryDayStranglePnl()'s output is points — an early framing of the Phase 3 exit-trigger design would have compared these directly, silently reintroducing the exact rupee-vs-points confusion class that originally motivated this project's entire three-gate risk-architecture redesign (the 2026-07-20 shouldExitSpread() incident).
- A value computed unconditionally at the top of a reactive flatMap block, but only actually needed inside one branch of a later if/else, still executes eagerly on every invocation — including branches where it's never used and its dependencies (e.g. a Mockito mock) may not be stubbed. Confirmed incident (2026-07-31, Expiry Day hedge Phase 3 session): strategiesConfig.getStrategy(...) was called unconditionally before an if/else that only needed it in the else (fallback) branch; in the common case where the primary branch was taken, the mock was unstubbed, causing a null-pointer that onErrorResume silently swallowed into Mono.empty() — surfacing only as a generic "expected onNext, got onComplete" StepVerifier failure, not an obvious value-mismatch error. Fix: make expensive/dependency-bearing computations lazy, evaluated only inside the branch that actually needs them.


## Important
These rules represent confirmed production bugs
from TradeMesh development history.
Each rule exists because a real bug was found.
Do not remove or ignore rules without understanding
why they were added.